       - 1. (M15) Relative Strength Index (RSI).  Think of the RSI as a speedometer that measures how fast car prices are moving, and whether buyers or sellers are getting tired. It ranges from a score of 0 to 100, 
            It tells if the market is over-pumped (Overbought) or completely exhausted (Oversold).
              Sell Condition : When Price rises into FVG/OB Zone, look for the RSI to climb above 70 and then cross back below 70 or 50, proving sellers are taking control.
              Buy Condition : When Price drops into  Of FVG/OB Zone, the RSI on the M15 chart will often drop below 30 (meaning sellers are exhausted). should look for the moment the RSI line crosses back above 30 or 50. This proves that buyers are stepping back in.
       - 2. (M15) Moving Average Convergence Divergence (MACD). Think of the MACD as a momentum camera. It doesn't look at individual price ticks; it looks at the overall shifting weight of the market,
            It consists of two lines and a bar chart (the histogram) that moves above and below a central "0" line.  It filters out "fake" price movements and tells you when the actual shift in power happens.
              Sell Condition : The easiest rule is the central 0 line. In A Sell Condition trade, wait for the MACD bars to switch from being above 0 (green/positive) to printing below 0 (red/negative).
              Buy Condition : The easiest rule is the central 0 line. In A Buy Condition trade, wait for the MACD bars to switch from being below 0 (red/negative) to printing above 0 (green/positive).
       - 3. (H1 And M15) Exponential Moving Average (EMA50). Think of the EMA 50 as a moving floor or ceiling. It takes the average price of the last 50 candles but puts more weight on the most recent candles so it reacts quickly to sudden news or movements.
            It acts as a visual trend barrier. If the price is above it, the immediate trend is up. If the price is below it, the immediate trend is down.
